---
/**
 * A3's Detail paragraph: the one place on the site where a bare paragraph of the specification's
 * copy is rendered, set beneath the cards on `/platform/site-intelligence` (AAP 0.8.1, 0.8.4).
 *
 * It borrows the template's detail-block anatomy and only its rhythm: an optional bracketed mono
 * eyebrow, body copy set directly on the page ground, then a single hairline closing the block
 * (AAP 0.6.2, design node 2221:568). There is deliberately no heading here and no block title, and
 * a later reader must not add one: `Props` carries no string for a title, and promoting the label
 * to a heading — or inventing one to sit under it — would put copy on the page that the
 * specification does not supply (AAP 0.7.6, rule B-4). The anatomy contributes the label, the
 * paragraph and the closing rule; it has no title slot this block has anything to fill.
 *
 * Nothing but that hairline contains the block: no card, no fill, no border box, no radius and no
 * shadow, because the design reference declares no elevation anywhere (AAP 0.2.2, 0.6.3). The
 * opening and closing y-rhythm is the block's own, and it is the design's: `space-60` above and
 * below at the narrow width and `space-80` at the wide one.
 *
 * Both strings arrive as props from `src/content/site-intelligence.ts`, so the block holds no copy
 * of its own, reads no content module, borrows nothing from the design file and invents nothing
 * (rule W-1, AAP 0.4.3). Nothing is configured or fetched here either: the block reads no build
 * configuration, names no address and reaches no remote origin (rules B-2, B-1).
 *
 * Presentation is the system's. `.content` is the page's content column, so the measure is 358
 * inside 16-pixel insets at 390 and 1344 inside 48-pixel insets at 1440. `.type-body-2` is the
 * intro-paragraph rung — Urbanist 400 at leading 1.5, 24 at the wide width and 14 at the narrow one
 * — and it steps with the theme's breakpoint rather than with anything declared here. The copy is
 * set ragged right: the design reference justifies the paragraph inside its own narrow measure, and
 * justification carried across this much wider column would open gaps between words that the
 * narrow setting never has. `.border-hairline` is the decorative 1-pixel Khaki rule the design
 * draws bottom-only across a block's full width; it never identifies a control, so it carries no
 * ink of its own beyond the rule. Chocolate on Cream measures 17.08:1, and the ink is set here
 * rather than inherited so the paragraph's contrast never depends on the ground a composer gives it
 * (rule B-3). The block declares no height, no clamp and no overflow, so copy that grows with the
 * reader's zoom or font size simply takes more lines.
 *
 * One static `<section>` holding at most two elements: no script, no framework import, no hydration
 * directive and no request (rules W-2, B-1), and nothing focusable, so the block adds no tab stop to
 * the page's keyboard path (rule B-3).
 */

import Label from './Label.astro';

interface Props {
  /**
   * A3's Detail paragraph, exactly as the content layer holds it and in full. It is rendered whole
   * and untouched — no case change, no truncation, no sentence split — so the copy a reader sees is
   * the specification's own (rule B-4, AAP 0.7.3).
   */
  text: string;
  /**
   * The block's bracketed eyebrow, when A3 gives it one. Absent — or blank after trimming, which is
   * how a field not yet filled arrives — renders nothing at all rather than an empty label and the
   * gap where it would have stood.
   */
  label?: string;
}

const { text, label } = Astro.props;

/**
 * The content column and the block's separation from the sections either side of it: `space-60` at
 * the narrow width and `space-80` at the wide one, the vertical padding the design gives this block
 * at each of its two artboards (AAP 0.8.4). The wide step comes from the theme's `--breakpoint-wide`
 * token through Tailwind's `wide:` variant, so the 900 breakpoint has one source (PA-5) and this
 * block moves with it. The column and the 16-pixel gap between the label and the paragraph — the
 * gap the design's eyebrow takes to the rung beneath it — are the pair's whole layout.
 */
const LAYOUT = 'content py-60 wide:py-80 flex flex-col gap-16';

/**
 * The paragraph: the body-2 rung, the page's primary ink, and the hairline that closes the block.
 * The padding below the copy is the space the design leaves between a block's last line and its
 * closing rule — `space-16` at the narrow width and `space-32` at the wide one — so the rule reads
 * as the block's edge rather than as an underline on the last sentence.
 */
const PARAGRAPH = 'type-body-2 text-ink-chocolate border-hairline pb-16 wide:pb-32';

/** The eyebrow as it will be rendered, kept exactly as the content layer wrote it. */
const labelText = label ?? '';

/**
 * A blank label counts as absent. Trimming decides that and nothing else: the string that reaches
 * the page is the caller's own, so the copy-fidelity comparison sees the specification's text.
 */
const hasLabel = labelText.trim() !== '';
---

<section class={LAYOUT}>
  {hasLabel && <Label text={labelText} tone="ink" />}
  <p class={PARAGRAPH}>{text}</p>
</section>
